    {
      "id": "library/10665-69034/blocks/prose-sec-005",
      "types": [
        "folio-assistant-core:Prose",
        "doco:Section"
      ],
      "kind": "prose",
      "title": "B. Immnization data quality self-assessment toolbox",
      "pageStart": 12,
      "pageEnd": 37,
      "target": "../sections/sec-005-b-immnization-data-quality-self-assessment-toolb.md",
      "narrative": null,
      "content": "2\n1.\nDQS options: overview\nThe DQS toolbox proposes several options to assess different aspects of the\nmonitoring system at different levels.\nTable 1. Description of the main areas a DQS can assess\nOption\nDistrict\nHealth Unit\nMain measures\nAssess reporting accuracy\nX\nX\nAccuracy ratio\nAssess recording accuracy\nX\nAccuracy ratio\n(sample in the community)\nAssess completeness/\nX\nX\nCompleteness of district reporting (%)\ntimeliness of reporting\nTimeliness of district reporting (%)\nDistrict report availability at national level (%)\nCompleteness of HU reporting (%)\nTimeliness of HU reporting (%)\nHU rep",
      "truncated": true,
      "provenance": "ingested",
      "summary": {
        "status": "draft",
        "text": "Presents the DQS toolbox options, summarised in a table of what can be assessed at district and health unit level. It explains in detail how to check reporting and recording accuracy by recounting immunizations across tally sheets, registers and reports and calculating accuracy ratios. Further sections cover completeness and timeliness of reporting, quality questions on the monitoring system, immunization card recording, vaccine wastage and safety monitoring. It ends with coverage denominators, which are explored through adaptable questions on quality.",
        "state": "draft",
        "draftedBy": {
          "kind": "agent",
          "id": "claude-code",
          "model": "not-disclosed"
        },
        "draftedAt": "2026-11-11"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "library/10665-69034/blocks/prose-sec-006",
      "types": [
        "folio-assistant-core:Prose",
        "doco:Section"
      ],
      "kind": "prose",
      "title": "C. Where to conduct a DQS?",
      "pageStart": 37,
      "pageEnd": 40,
      "target": "../sections/sec-006-c-where-to-conduct-a-dqs.md",
      "narrative": null,
      "content": "WHO/IVB/05.04\nIdeally a DQS should be introduced in a country and conducted on an ongoing basis.\nIn a district though, the number of sites to be visited is constrained by the desired\nprecision and logistic issues, such as time available, the number of team members,\ndistances, ease of travel, the ability to obtain security clearance in difficult areas, and\nthe availability of vehicles, drivers and accommodation.\nA choice of sites to be visited must be made. However, the greater the sample size,\nthe more precise the results will be.\nTo provide a reasonable idea of the situation in one district,",
      "truncated": true,
      "provenance": "ingested",
      "summary": {
        "status": "draft",
        "text": "Advises visiting at least three and at most six health units per district, since precision gains level off beyond six, and stresses that discussion of findings matters more than exact figures. It discusses how to select districts and health units and notes that results from one district cannot be extrapolated, though common problems can be shared through feedback.",
        "state": "draft",
        "draftedBy": {
          "kind": "agent",
          "id": "claude-code",
          "model": "not-disclosed"
        },
        "draftedAt": "2026-11-11"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "library/10665-69034/blocks/prose-sec-007",
      "types": [
        "folio-assistant-core:Prose",
        "doco:Section"
      ],
      "kind": "prose",
      "title": "D. Present the DQS findings",
      "pageStart": 40,
      "pageEnd": 44,
      "target": "../sections/sec-007-d-present-the-dqs-findings.md",
      "narrative": null,
      "content": "30\nThe data quality assessment provides a certain amount of information on the status\nof records and practices related to the reporting system.\nAll options in the DQS toolbox provide quantitative measures which can be followed\neasily over time and used to compare different areas. The use of the tool will be\nparticularly interesting when several districts can be compared or a district can be\ncompared to itself over time.\nAssessment findings should be presented and discussed to the level that was assessed\nbut also to the national level so that lessons can be drawn and solutions proposed for\nthe",
      "truncated": true,
      "provenance": "ingested",
      "summary": {
        "status": "draft",
        "text": "Explains how to present DQS findings, using accuracy ratios in tables and bar charts with worked examples, and how to compare districts or the same district over time. It also covers presenting completeness, timeliness, quality index scores and wastage results, and mentions supporting spreadsheet workbooks.",
        "state": "draft",
        "draftedBy": {
          "kind": "agent",
          "id": "claude-code",
          "model": "not-disclosed"
        },
        "draftedAt": "2026-11-11"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "library/10665-69034/blocks/prose-sec-008",
      "types": [
        "folio-assistant-core:Prose",
        "doco:Section"
      ],
      "kind": "prose",
      "title": "E. Conduct a DQS workshop",
      "pageStart": 44,
      "pageEnd": 47,
      "target": "../sections/sec-008-e-conduct-a-dqs-workshop.md",
      "narrative": null,
      "content": "34\nThe suggested approach is to introduce the DQS in a country through a national\nworkshop, followed immediately by an assessment in a number of districts and HUs.\nThe workshop aims:\n•\nto sensitize key health personnel on the importance of accurately monitoring\nimmunization activities and data quality;\n•\nto train key health personnel from the national and district levels in data quality\nself-assessment methodology (theoretical and practical);\n•\nto enable the participants to conduct a data quality assessment in a number of\ndistricts and facilities of the country immediately after the workshop;",
      "truncated": true,
      "provenance": "ingested",
      "summary": {
        "status": "draft",
        "text": "Recommends introducing the DQS through a national workshop followed straight away by field assessments, and lists the workshop aims. In the first phase participants review monitoring tools and design and test their own assessment, and in the second they carry out fieldwork before reconvening to analyse results and make recommendations. It also advises involving local partners and academic institutions to sustain the work.",
        "state": "draft",
        "draftedBy": {
          "kind": "agent",
          "id": "claude-code",
          "model": "not-disclosed"
        },
        "draftedAt": "2026-11-11"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "library/10665-69034/blocks/prose-sec-009",
      "types": [
        "folio-assistant-core:Prose",
        "doco:Section"
      ],
      "kind": "prose",
      "title": "F. Integrate DQS results into the routine activities",
      "pageStart": 47,
      "pageEnd": 51,
      "target": "../sections/sec-009-f-integrate-dqs-results-into-the-routine-activit.md",
      "narrative": null,
      "content": "WHO/IVB/05.04\nThe key to DQS success depends on the success of the workshop and first assessment.\nA well-focused assessment should result in the following.\n•\nA documented monitoring system: The tool should help managers to estimate\nwhether the information collected is reliable (accurate), and whether the\ninformation is properly used (the monitoring system is of good quality).\n•\nIdentified weaknesses and strengths of the system: Major problems should\nbe localized.\n•\nRecommendations for improvement of the performance of the system:\nMonitoring immunization services is meaningful only if the infor",
      "truncated": true,
      "provenance": "ingested",
      "summary": {
        "status": "draft",
        "text": "Describes what a successful assessment should produce, such as a documented monitoring system, identified strengths and weaknesses, recommendations used to adjust microplans and supervision, and a follow-up plan. It discusses ways to build the self-designed tool into routine practice, and the block ends with part of an example monitoring chart of DTP doses and drop-out rates.",
        "state": "draft",
        "draftedBy": {
          "kind": "agent",
          "id": "claude-code",
          "model": "not-disclosed"
        },
        "draftedAt": "2026-11-11"
      }
    },
